// Data model: theorems of category theory (MSC 2020 18) and the premises of one usual textbook proof of each: general
// theory, limits and adjunctions (18A), topoi (18B), monads (18C), abelian categories (18E), sheaves and Grothendieck
// topologies (18F), homological algebra (18G), monoidal categories (18M) and higher categories (18N).
//
// Model (see reports/2026-11-03 Satzgraphen aller Gebiete/auftrag.md): id, name, kind, area, field, msc, msc5,
// premises, formula (without quantifiers), text, proof, concepts, year, who. `area` is the five-character MSC class
// (`msc5`) or "basic" for results from areas without a home folder (modules, 16D). `premises` contains only the direct
// premises of the proof named in `proof`; references to other folders have a prefix ("st:zorn-lemma"). `concepts` are
// the concepts of ./concepts.js that occur in the statement (prefixed ones from other folders). `premises` and `proof`
// are the standard proof (name in `proofName`, optional `proofYear`, `proofWho`); further proofs in `alternativeProofs`
// ({ name, premises, idea, year, who }); `foundation: true` marks general tools (3 November 2026).
//
// Prefix for references from other folders: "ct:<id>".
// Sources: Mac Lane, Categories for the Working Mathematician (2nd ed. 1998); Riehl, Category Theory in Context (2016);
// Weibel, An Introduction to Homological Algebra (1994); Mac Lane and Moerdijk, Sheaves in Geometry and Logic (1992);
// Grothendieck, Sur quelques points d'algèbre homologique (Tôhoku 1957); Freyd, Abelian Categories (1964);
// Hovey, Model Categories (1999); Lurie, Higher Topos Theory (2009). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory. tests/check-theorems.js warns about theorems without
// premises that have neither mark.

